The centers of the two circles lie along the $y$-axis. Without loss of generality, let the coordinates of the center of the right circle be $(0,y).$  Then the right circle has equation
\[x^2 + (y - r)^2 = r^2.\]Since the right circle is tangent to the ellipse, when $y = -r,$ the equation above reduces to
\[x^2 = 6r,\]so $x = \pm \sqrt{6r}.$

By rotating the coordinate system, setting $y = -r,$ and solving for the tangent point, the resulting right triangle has vertices  $(0,0),$ $\left(\sqrt{6r},-r\right),$ and $(\sqrt{6r},0).$  By the Pythagorean Theorem,
\[r^2 + (\sqrt{6r})^2 = (r + \sqrt{6r})^2.\]This simplifies to $r^2 - 2r - 6 = 0.$  By the Quadratic Formula, $r = 1 \pm \sqrt{7}.$

Since $r > 0,$ $r = \boxed{1 - \sqrt{7}}.$ </think> <answer> 1 - \sqrt{7} </answer>

