 Since all three numbers are less than 1, they are sides of a triangle if the longest side is less than the sum of the other two.  If $a$ is the longest side, so that $a = \max(x,y,1)$, there are three cases: $0 \le y < x < a < 1$; $0 \le x < y < a < 1$; and $0 < x < y < a = 1$.  We can use these three regions to us to analyze the problem.  When $y < x < a < 1$, the triangle relation gives $a < x + y$.  This can be rewritten as $x < y + 1 - a$ or $x + y < 1$.  When $y < x < 1 < a$, the inequality $a - x - y \ge 0$ holds.  When $y < 1 < x < 1$, there is clearly a triangle, as well as when $1 < x < y < 1$.

These analysis allow us to combine all the cases to conclude: \begin{align*}
1 - p &= \int_0^1 \left( \int_0^x \left( \int_0^{1 - x - y} dx \right) dy \right) + \int_0^1 \left( \int_A^{1 - x + A} dx \right) dy \\
&+ \int_1^y \int_0^x dx dy + \int_1^y \int_A^1 dx dy.
\end{align*}The domains $A \le x \le 1 - y + A$ and $A \ge x \ge 0$ must be some of the segments of area of regions $E$ shown in the figure below: [asy]
unitsize(1.5cm);
real A = 3/4;
